<h1>Fortress: the revival so far</h1>
</header>

<details class="words" id="glossary">
<summary><strong class="w-head">Glossary</strong> <span class="w-lead">The words below have one meaning on this page. Skim them if a term is new to you.</span></summary>
<dl class="gloss">
<div><dt>batch</dt><dd>Set of tasks that agents carry out at once and land together.</dd></div>
<div><dt>checker</dt><dd>Compiler's static type checker.</dd></div>
<div><dt>checker count</dt><dd>Checker's errors on <code>FortressLibrary</code>, up to the first stage with an error.</dd></div>
<div><dt>closed trait</dt><dd>Trait with a <code>comprises</code> clause.</dd></div>
<div><dt>code generator</dt><dd>Part of the compiler that writes bytecode.</dd></div>
<div><dt>coercion</dt><dd>Declared conversion from one type into another.</dd></div>
<div><dt>compiler</dt><dd>Team's implementation that compiles to JVM bytecode.</dd></div>
<div><dt>component</dt><dd>File of Fortress code that implements apis.</dd></div>
<div><dt>core</dt><dd>Components of the library that every program loads.</dd></div>
<div><dt>curator</dt><dd>Person in charge of the revival.</dd></div>
<div><dt>dispatch</dt><dd>Run-time choice among overloads.</dd></div>
<div><dt>distance</dt><dd>Checker's errors on the core, with every stage run.</dd></div>
<div><dt>exclude</dt><dd>Relation of two types that no value has together.</dd></div>
<div><dt>functional method</dt><dd>Method with <code>self</code> among its parameters, called as <code>f(x)</code>.</dd></div>
<div><dt>gap</dt><dd>Something missing or broken.</dd></div>
<div><dt>gap ledger</dt><dd>File with one numbered row for each known gap.</dd></div>
<div><dt>gate</dt><dd>Full build and test of a batch before it lands.</dd></div>
<div><dt>instantiation rule</dt><dd>Rule that no type extends two instantiations of one generic trait.</dd></div>
<div><dt>interpreter</dt><dd>Team's implementation that runs programs without static types.</dd></div>
<div><dt>library</dt><dd>Interpreter's library.</dd></div>
<div><dt>microGPT</dt><dd>Karpathy's small GPT, in two Fortress programs.</dd></div>
<div><dt>native binding</dt><dd>Library function whose body is Java code.</dd></div>
<div><dt>phase</dt><dd>One of the six parts of the curator's plan.</dd></div>
<div><dt>quick check</dt><dd>Short run of microGPT under the interpreter.</dd></div>
<div><dt>reading</dt><dd>One of several interpretations of a rule.</dd></div>
<div><dt>record</dt><dd>Project's notes.</dd></div>
<div><dt>reduction</dt><dd>Library object that combines the values of a big operator.</dd></div>
<div><dt>self-typed trait</dt><dd>Trait whose parameter stands for its implementing type.</dd></div>
<div><dt>siblings</dt><dd>Number types of which none is a subtype of another.</dd></div>
<div><dt>stage</dt><dd>One kind of check in the checker's sequence.</dd></div>
<div><dt>static parameter</dt><dd>Type or size parameter of a declaration.</dd></div>
<div><dt>switch-over</dt><dd>Step at which the compiler starts to use the library.</dd></div>
<div><dt>tower</dt><dd>Number types in which each is a subtype of the next.</dd></div>
<div><dt>type group</dt><dd>Part of the team that built the checker and the compiler.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Working Draft</dt><dd>Team's last full specification, of 2 February 2011.</dd></div>
</dl>
</details>
<section id="fortress-the-revival-and-who-does-the-work" class="part">
<h2>1. Fortress, the revival and who does the work</h2>
<p>Fortress was an experimental language for high-performance computing. A team at Sun Labs designed and built it from 2003 to 2012, led by Guy L. Steele Jr. and Eric Allen. Sukyoung Ryu, David Chase, Jan-Willem Maessen and Christine Flood were at its core. On this page, "the team" means that group. The <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress">repository's README</a> tells its history.</p>
<p>The team's lab, by then Oracle Labs, ended the project in summer 2012. Its hardest problem was still open: instantiating generic code at run time at a low cost. The team left a specification with features that it never built, an interpreter, and a partial compiler to JVM bytecode. By 2026 that code no longer built on current Java.</p>
<p>The revival began on 18 August 2026. It finishes the team's design and doesn't redesign it. Its aim is one model, Andrej Karpathy's microGPT, a small GPT in Python. The revival wants it compiled from Fortress to JVM bytecode, and as fast as a version written in plain Java.</p>
<p>In Fortress, microGPT is two programs. The flat program holds the model's data in flat, unnested arrays, after Aaron Hsu's style of APL. The APL program is written in an APL notation, which a grammar that the program imports turns into Fortress code.</p>
<p>AI agents do the work: sessions of Anthropic's Claude, each started with written instructions for one task. They write the code, the tests and the project's notes, and one of them coordinates the others. The curator, the person in charge of the revival, directs them. He sets the goals and their order, decides what the team's sources leave open, and approves each step of the work before it starts.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-rule-and-the-teams-sources" class="part">
<h2>2. The rule and the team's sources</h2>
<h3>The rule</h3>
<p>One rule judges every change, the revival's first: "We are custodians of their language, not its authors." The revival finishes what the team intended. It adds a design of its own only where the team's sources leave a question open, and then the curator decides it. Four practices follow from the rule:</p>
<ul>
<li>The revival changes the team's Fortress for one of two reasons only: two of the team's sources contradict each other, or a program fails.</li>
<li>Where two sources contradict each other, the team's later choice wins (below).</li>
<li>An agent checks every claim against the team's sources or a command's output.</li>
<li>No committed file claims credit for the revival. Where git doesn't record who wrote something, an agent works it out from the history and never guesses.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The team's three sources</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The specification.</strong> The team published version 1.0 in March 2008, and its last full text is the Working Draft of 2 February 2011. The repository keeps that text unchanged, beside a copy that agents revise.</li>
<li><strong>The type papers.</strong> The type group, the part of the team that built the type checker and the compiler, wrote them. The chief ones are the OOPSLA 2011 paper of Allen and others, Karl Naden's notes of 2012, and the POPL 2019 paper of Park, Hong, Steele and Ryu.</li>
<li><strong>The code.</strong> It holds two implementations, each with its own library. The interpreter, run by <code>fortress walk</code>, runs a program without static types. The compiler, run by <code>fortress compile</code>, checks a program's types with its type checker, here called the checker, and writes JVM bytecode. From here on, "the library" alone means the interpreter's library.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How a contradiction is decided</h3>
<p>The team's later choice wins over its earlier one, in a text or in code. Code counts as a choice where the team built it the same way for all its types. It also counts where the team stated its rule in a commit message, a comment or the checker. Code that its author called a stub, or that works around a limit of the JVM, doesn't count.</p>
<p>Much of the Working Draft came before the implementations. The type group later learned what could be built and made fast, and put that into the type papers and the compiler's library. So the revival follows those where they depart from the Working Draft. Where two later choices conflict, or the sources settle nothing, the curator decides by the revival's aim: microGPT compiled and fast.</p>
<p>An agent marks each changed passage of the specification with a box headed "Revised by the 2026 revival", as the team marked its own changes. The box points to an entry in Appendix I, which gives the change, its reason and the original text.</p>
</section>
<section id="how-the-work-runs" class="part">
<h2>3. How the work runs</h2>
<h3>Components and the plan</h3>
<p>Fortress code comes in components. A component is a file of code that implements apis, its interfaces, and imports the apis that it uses. The library's core is the components that a program imports by default, and the components that those import. Every program that the interpreter runs loads the core, and so will every compiled program once the compiler uses the library.</p>
<p>On 26 September the curator approved a plan of six phases. Phases 1 and 2 carried out work that he had already decided. The other four:</p>
<ol start="3">
<li>The checker reports no error on the library's core.</li>
<li>The switch-over, the step at which the compiler starts to use the library and its own library is deleted, and the work it needs first.</li>
<li>microGPT compiles. This needs its static types, the design of its arrays, and the end of the code generator's gaps. The code generator is the part of the compiler that writes bytecode, and a gap is something missing or broken. Phase 5 also takes on the team's open problem: the compiler will instantiate generic code at run time by the POPL 2019 paper's rule.</li>
<li>microGPT runs fast, with unboxed numbers, measured against the plain Java version.</li>
</ol>
<p>A native binding, the subject of much of phase 4, is a library function whose body is Java code.</p>
<h3>A batch</h3>
<p>The agents work in batches. A batch is a set of tasks that agents carry out at the same time and land together. To land is to commit to <code>main</code> and push it.</p>
<p>Batches are numbered in sequence. A letter or a decimal, as in 6b or 6.5, marks a batch added between two numbers. So a batch's number is not a count. Each batch has seven steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>One agent drafts the batch: its tasks, their reasons, and the curator's questions with proposed defaults.</li>
<li>The curator reads the draft and approves it.</li>
<li>An agent does each task in its own copy of the repository, writing a failing test first.</li>
<li>A second agent reads each change against the specification, the decisions and the team's code, and repairs what it finds. A third settles any dispute.</li>
<li>One agent applies the changes to <code>main</code> and writes the findings into the record: the project's notes on what it has established, decided and measured.</li>
<li>The gate runs: a full build and test of the combined changes. If it passes, the batch lands.</li>
<li>An agent reviews the landed batch against the team's intent, and lists what it left open.</li>
</ol>
<p>The gate runs both implementations' test suites and counts the two measures below. After the gate, the quick check runs the flat and the APL program under the interpreter for a few training steps, against reference values.</p>
<h3>The two measures</h3>
<p>Two numbers measure how far the checker is from the switch-over. Both count its errors on the library's core. The checker works in stages: it runs one kind of check after another.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The checker count</strong> reads one component, <code>FortressLibrary</code>, with the apis that it imports. The checker stops at the first stage that reports an error in a component, so the count misses the errors of the later stages. A repair that lets the checker reach a later stage can therefore raise the count. It began at about 100 errors on 23 September.</li>
<li><strong>The distance</strong>, short for the distance to the switch-over, counts every error in the core, which it has covered whole since 30 October. It runs every stage, whatever the earlier stages found, so a repair can't raise it by uncovering errors. It began at 1.7K errors on 27 September.</li>
</ul>
<p>The measures matter because the compiler can start to use the library only once its checker finds no error in the core. The plan repairs the library's other components, outside both measures, before the first program that needs each one.</p>
<p>A reading is one interpretation of a rule where the specification's text allows more than one. A measure's zero can depend on one reading. Then the curator decides whether that reading stands.</p>
</section>
