<section id="what-happened" class="part">
<h2>5. What happened</h2>
<h3>Running the 2012 code again, 18 August to 16 September</h3>
<p>In August an agent made the team's code build again on current Java and Scala, with no change a Fortress programmer sees.</p>
<p>Agents then ported microGPT to Fortress and ran it under the interpreter. The first port took 10.9 s for one training step, against 22 ms in Python. The interpreter is the team's reference implementation, not a fast one, so the curator judges speed on the compiler only.</p>
<p>Later ports, each in a fresh session, listed the gaps they met, and the flat and the APL program came out of them.</p>
<p>An agent put the gaps into the gap ledger, a file with one numbered row for each known gap. The ledger, a list of facts and a list of the curator's decisions were the first parts of the record.</p>
<h3>Before the plan, to 26 September: seven batches</h3>
<p>The first batches added missing names to the compiler's library, so that more of the team's test programs compile and run.</p>
<p>On 21 September the curator chose one library for both implementations: the interpreter's, the fuller one. Making the checker accept it costs less than porting it into the compiler's library, which the switch-over deletes. From then on, batches removed the checker's errors on the library instead of adding to the compiler's library.</p>
<p>The next batches started on those errors. One of them gave the interpreter coercion, a declared conversion from one type into another. The specification says that a call converts its arguments when that makes it apply. At that place the interpreter had a note, "TODO add checks for COERCE". The interpreter now converts at calls, typed bindings and assignments.</p>
<h3>Phases 1 and 2, 26 to 29 September: five batches</h3>
<p>The team's sources contradicted each other on the number types. <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code> and <code>ZZ</code> are the 32-bit, the 64-bit and the unbounded integers.</p>
<ul>
<li>The team's interpreter library put them in a tower: <code>ZZ32</code> was a subtype of <code>ZZ64</code>, and <code>ZZ64</code> of <code>ZZ</code>.</li>
<li>In 2009 the compiler's library made them siblings, of which none is a subtype of another. Sukyoung Ryu wrote its first exclusion, <code>ZZ32 excludes ZZ64</code>: no value has both types. Maessen and Eric Allen added coercions, such as the one from <code>ZZ32</code> into <code>ZZ64</code>.</li>
<li>Justin Hilburn built the instantiation rule into the checker: no type may extend two instantiations of one generic trait. In the tower, <code>ZZ32</code> is an <code>Integral[\ZZ32\]</code> and, through <code>ZZ64</code>, an <code>Integral[\ZZ64\]</code>, so the rule refuses it. The OOPSLA 2011 paper states the rule; the Working Draft doesn't.</li>
</ul>
<p>The return-type rule says that the more specific of two overloads returns a subtype of the other's return type. The curator kept the instantiation rule for two reasons. It's the team's later choice, which the POPL 2019 paper keeps. And without it, the checker's return-type rule passes programs that fail with <code>IncompatibleClassChangeError</code>. So agents made the library's number types siblings, as in the compiler's library, and revised the specification's text on numbers.</p>
<p>Phase 1 carried out two decisions. A Fortress type can carry a size, such as an array's length, through a size parameter, written <code>nat</code>. The compiler couldn't carry a size at run time, though the team's code generator already wrote an empty field for it in each run-time type descriptor. An agent filled that field in the team's pattern for type arguments, so a program with sizes now compiles and runs.</p>
<p>The second decision concerns overflow. The specification and the compiler raise <code>IntegerOverflow</code> when a 32-bit or 64-bit integer result doesn't fit, but the interpreter wrapped the result. Now the interpreter raises the error too. Code meant to wrap uses the specification's own wrapping operators, ∔, ∸ and ⨰.</p>
<h3>Phase 3, 28 September to 30 October: the checker at zero, 22 batches</h3>
<p>The aim was a zero in both measures. The errors were defects of the checker, small errors in the library, and contradictions between the sources. Agents repaired the first two kinds, and settled most contradictions for the type group's later work.</p>
<p><strong>Overloading.</strong> The largest group of decisions follows the OOPSLA 2011 paper. It lets two overloads differ only in their static parameters: the type and size parameters, such as <code>T</code> in <code>Box[\T\]</code>. One sentence of the Working Draft forbids such pairs, but neither implementation enforced it, and the library has many of them. So the curator kept the paper's rule, and an agent revised the sentence. Three more changes follow the paper:</p>
<ul>
<li>The checker tested the return-type rule at one instantiation of a generic declaration, so some programs passed it and failed the JVM's verification of the bytecode. Now it tests every instantiation.</li>
<li>Two generic declarations may coexist where no argument fits both, even if no single parameter type excludes its counterpart.</li>
<li>The checker checks symbolic operators such as <code>+</code>, which it had skipped, a gap that the specification's appendix of proposed features names.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Inference.</strong> Where a call determines no type for a type parameter, both implementations gave it the empty type <code>Bottom</code>, and some compiled calls then failed the JVM's verification. Following the POPL 2019 paper, the checker now gives such a parameter its bound, and so does the interpreter in almost every case.</p>
<p><strong>Self types.</strong> A self-typed trait is one whose type parameter stands for the type that implements it, as <code>I</code> in <code>Integral[\I\]</code>. The checker now types <code>self</code> in such a trait as the implementing type, a rule that Scala also has. So it accepts the library's integer traits as the team wrote them.</p>
<p>The team's vectors are additive groups, and the library also adds an array and a number. The checker refused these overloads of <code>+</code>, though no call fits both. The curator kept the vectors as the team wrote them. The checker's test of exclusion now reads the self type, which shows that the overloads exclude each other.</p>
<p><strong>The team's later code.</strong> The Working Draft gives an integer power a rational value, which the team never built. The team's later code gives the power its base's type, and the library now follows it. At a negative exponent only 1 and -1 give a power, so a fraction needs a float base, as in <code>2.0^(-1)</code>.</p>
<p>An array's index type is bounded by a trait <code>Index</code>, which ranges exclude, as the specification's appendix of proposed features asks. So a subscript by one index and a subscript by a range never apply to the same value.</p>
<p><strong>Decided by the curator.</strong> The sources didn't settle two questions, and the curator decided them by the revival's aim:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ranges took any integer type in the interpreter's library, and only <code>ZZ32</code> in the compiler's. They're now over <code>ZZ32</code> only, because a JVM array holds at most as many elements as the largest <code>ZZ32</code>.</li>
<li>For an index such as <code>m[i, j]</code>, the team asked for "a magic" to convert the tuple into an <code>Index</code>, and named no way to do it. A tuple of <code>Index</code> types is now itself an <code>Index</code>, which changes no value. This departs from the team's text on tuples, and the specification says so.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>The close.</strong> Both measures reached 0 on 24 October. The phase's last batch closed it on 30 October with three changes, and both stayed at 0:</p>
<ul>
<li>The checker compares each api with its component at every member and operator. A compiled call of a member that the component lacks fails at run time.</li>
<li>The compile command bounds a type parameter without a written bound by <code>Any</code>, the type of every value. The Working Draft says <code>Object</code>, but the library uses its generic containers with tuples, which <code>Object</code> doesn't hold.</li>
<li>The distance covers the whole core, because <code>List</code>, a core component, compiles only with the components that it imports.</li>
</ul>
<p>The distance's zero holds under one reading, which the curator has still to decide. An object's value parameter is a field set by its constructor, and <code>var</code> makes it changeable. Under this reading, a component's object may add <code>var</code> to a value parameter that its api declares without it. Under the other reading, the distance finds one error.</p>
<h3>Phase 4, from 30 October: before the switch-over, open</h3>
<p>The curator set the phase's order: the native bindings first, then the switch-over. Work on the code generator goes on beside that order, since the compiler must then write bytecode for the library, not only check it.</p>
<p>Each native binding named a Java class of the interpreter, which takes the interpreter's boxed values, and compiled code can't pass those. So each binding now names a static Java method over plain Java values, which both implementations call. Fortress has no foreign-function interface, so the binding keeps the library's own form: a call of <code>builtinPrimitive</code> with the method's name.</p>
<p>The first batch of the phase built that form; later batches move the bindings group by group.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-changes-by-area" class="part">
<h2>6. The changes by area</h2>
<p>Section 5 tells the main changes with their reasons. This section adds the others, by area, at phase 3's close.</p>
<h3>Numbers</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> are each one generic declaration over the operators of the element type. The team's <code>SUM</code> relied on operators that <code>Number</code> declared for any two numbers, and the sibling number types have none.</li>
<li>An integer numeral has its own type, <code>IntLiteral</code>, which the number types convert from. This is the compiler library's design, since the specification's numeral types were unfinished. The interpreter still types a numeral by its value.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Order and reductions</h3>
<ul>
<li>A list is ordered only where its element type is totally ordered. So to the checker a list of <code>RR64</code>, the 64-bit floats, has no <code>&lt;</code>, because NaN compares with nothing.</li>
<li>A big operator, such as <code>SUM</code>, applies an operator across a range of values. A reduction is the library object behind it, which combines the values. The checker can't infer a reduction's element type from its clauses. So the library writes the type, and so must a program, as in <code>SUM[\ZZ32\][j &lt;- 0#n] f(j)</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The interpreter</h3>
<p>Three terms first. A functional method is a method with <code>self</code> among its parameters, called as <code>f(x)</code>. A closed trait is a trait with a <code>comprises</code> clause, which lists the types that its values belong to. The meet of two overloads is the intersection of their parameter types.</p>
<ul>
<li>Among the declarations that fit a call, the interpreter now prefers the more specific by their declared types, as the specification says. It no longer goes by their order in the file.</li>
<li>When it loads a program, it refuses what breaks the specification's rules on overloading and on closed traits, as the checker does. Examples are an object that extends two closed traits without being listed in them, and two functional methods with no declaration on their meet.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The checker and the code generator</h3>
<ul>
<li>The checker checks sizes, also sums and products in a size, because the team's arrays carry their sizes in their types.</li>
<li>A <code>comprises</code> clause speaks of values: every value of the closed trait is a value of a listed type. Some passages of the Working Draft read it as a statement about types, and are then false. The team's partial restart of the specification, begun in 2012, reads it by values, and the revival follows it.</li>
<li>A top-level <code>var</code> compiles, as some of the team's test programs need, and an <code>atomic</code> block, Fortress's transaction, covers it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Left as the team had it</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dispatch by the run-time types of every argument, and functional methods.</li>
<li>Juxtaposition as an operator, the rules on whitespace, and the grammar. The APL notation comes from a grammar that a program imports, a feature that the team built.</li>
<li>Loops and reductions are library code: a <code>for</code> loop and <code>SUM</code> become calls of a generator, which produces the values, and of a reduction.</li>
<li>Evaluation is parallel by default. Only the compiler checks the types of expressions; the interpreter's checks at load concern declarations.</li>
<li>What the specification describes and the team never built is still unbuilt: dimensions and units, <code>property</code> declarations, regions and complex numbers.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section id="the-record" class="part">
<h2>7. The record</h2>
<p>The record is the project's notes, kept in the repository beside the team's code. Its main parts are the gap ledger (section 5), the list of facts, the list of the curator's decisions, and an index of all notes. An agent reads the parts its task needs before it starts, so no agent works out again what the record holds.</p>
<figure><img src="record-growth.png" alt="The gap ledger&#x27;s characters and rows, and FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX, commit by commit from 09-08 to 11-06, with each batch&#x27;s landing marked" width="1080" height="1620"></figure>
<p>The chart has three panels on one time axis, with a vertical line at each batch's landing. They show the ledger's characters, its rows, and the sizes of the facts, the decisions and the index. The ledger grows mostly while batches run, and the index by one line for each new note. Each sharp fall is a rewrite that shortened the entries, with the earlier text kept in history.</p>
</section>
